  "body": "## What are you trying to do\n\nPost a rectangular wire EDM punch to an Agie Classic Cut 2S from the desktop or a command script. The controller needs a linked ISO contour, SBR resource list and SBL job. The generic ISO wire post writes a single program.\n\n## How you imagine it working\n\nSelect the native post and a compatible machine. Select the controller separation length explicitly. Write all three files to a new job directory. Refuse an unsupported setup without creating output. The controller applies its resident wire compensation to the nominal contour.\n\nThe proposed first case is one vertical rectangular punch in 20 mm stock, with a 0.25 mm wire and a 1 mm separation cut. An offline control accepted a synthetic 40 by 20 mm example. Its graphical check showed a rough pass, three trims and a final separation sequence. This was a software check. A physical machine was not tested.\n\n## Source decision\n\nThe proposed writer draws on an independently developed MIT-licensed implementation. Its source notes attribute some interface details to observations of controller examples. The native job uses a resident technology selection. We have not verified a public programming manual for all required call arguments and selection values. No vendor runtime, control image or technology table is proposed for distribution.\n\nDoes this reference meet the project's clean-room source rules for an EDM post? If a different reference is required, which published programming reference should we use?\n\nDone means: The project accepts the sources for the post. The desktop and command script produce the same complete native job. Tests pin the supported output, refuse unsupported setups, and preserve an existing destination.\n",
